#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128768 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "344f1838-a027-5fd5-82da-17e9b4fcac2b" :knowledge-point/title "Further Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "b94f1140-03a8-541b-9401-7456412fcd92" :question/math-academy-id "e-10071" :question/problem "The area $A$ of a forest decreases with time due to deforestation. The rate of change of the forest area is proportional to the square of the area. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the area at time $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **area** $A$ of a forest **decreases** with time due to deforestation. The **rate** of change of the forest area is **proportional** to the **area squared**.  *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}$ is *proportional to* the *area squared.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} \\propto A^2.\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\propto$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the area *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = -kA^2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128769 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d6e417b-e2a7-57f1-8dcd-4de5ff5d7e6b" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Inversely Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "3c8ddbe7-4a53-5340-bab4-1ffdb602e155" :question/math-academy-id "e-10073" :question/problem "The volume of water $V$ in a bucket decreases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the square of the volume of water. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the volume of water $V?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The  **volume of water** $V$  in a bucket **decreases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the ** square of the volume of water** $V.$*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume of water* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *square of the volume.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} \\propto \\dfrac{1}{V^2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\propto$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the volume of water *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} = -\\dfrac{k}{V^2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128871 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a247a205-ca5c-5079-820c-cfa34b6f53db" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Constant Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "725a3b25-e8ad-501f-9b32-3f4bc42e2c8e" :question/math-academy-id "e-521" :question/problem "A helium balloon loses $20$ grams of helium every second. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the mass $M$ of the balloon?" :question/worked-solution "If the mass of the balloon is $M$ grams, then the rate of change of the mass is $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}M}{\\textrm{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in seconds.\n\nSince $20$ grams are lost every second, the rate of change of the balloon's mass is given by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}M}{\\textrm{d}t}=-20.\n$$\n\nNotice that the right-hand side is *negative* because the balloon is *losing* mass."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128872 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "255dee12-5f44-536b-b679-5a894843395d" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "2f48bc21-3bd4-5dd1-8b60-89121591bfed" :question/math-academy-id "e-522" :question/problem "The volume of water $V(t)$ in a leaking tank decreases at a rate that is proportional to the time $t$ (in minutes) since the leak started. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the volume of water at time $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of water $V(t)$ in a leaking tank **decreases** at a **rate** that is **proportional** to the **time** $t$ (in minutes) since the leak started. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume* of water varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}$ is *proportional to* the *time* since the water begins to leak.  In other words,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} \\propto t.\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\propto$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the volume of water *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} = -kt.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313723 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a247a205-ca5c-5079-820c-cfa34b6f53db" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Constant Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce12ebed-a77d-55f7-90f8-22c5b9dfbf8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313725} {:answer/id #uuid "821be48c-6c28-599a-a1df-cf3418d1bb86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=6P" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313726} {:answer/id #uuid "af92d1b9-2507-5e94-9abd-fb632d45b1b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=P-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313727} {:answer/id #uuid "fa3d769c-c627-57a0-b301-05bcabe5649d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313728} {:answer/id #uuid "ba2d3d44-c6a4-552b-af23-bf2cc078ffa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=-6P" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce12ebed-a77d-55f7-90f8-22c5b9dfbf8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313725} :answer-field/id #uuid "5108b9bd-46b0-53ff-a9c8-fbd4081c54ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313724}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac92dfbf-ccb9-575c-9ce8-ae4ff8a7a4a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-119053" :question/problem "Lucy reads $6$ pages of her new book each day. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the number of pages $P(t)$ Lucy has left to read? Assume that the time $t$ is measured in days." :question/worked-solution "If the number of pages Lucy has left to read is $P$, then the rate of change of the number of the pages is $\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in days.\n\nSince Lucy reads $6$ pages every day, the rate of change of the number of pages she has left is given by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=-6.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is negative because the number of pages Lucy has left to read is *decreasing*."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313730 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "344f1838-a027-5fd5-82da-17e9b4fcac2b" :knowledge-point/title "Further Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd86361b-c2b2-57c1-8939-9d91a4174c60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=k{S}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313732} {:answer/id #uuid "58a97aba-52e7-5dc6-9464-3f0c8f478328" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-k\\sqrt{S},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313733} {:answer/id #uuid "8028e7e8-3b3b-504d-ba91-f836d9cb7c39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=k\\sqrt{S},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313734} {:answer/id #uuid "22454eb9-32f0-562a-a6d6-34138d61dbee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-k{S}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313735} {:answer/id #uuid "444c2823-b849-5936-a4d8-fe451fc18875" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{S}^{2}},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd86361b-c2b2-57c1-8939-9d91a4174c60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=k{S}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313732} :answer-field/id #uuid "18d74731-e3b8-56d6-ac10-f73636d8a859" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313731}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "95e26d6b-7e34-50c6-b783-26e5d7040c15" :question/math-academy-id "q-143031" :question/problem "The number of sales $S$ of a particular book increases with time. The rate of change of the number of sales is proportional to the square of the number of sales. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the number of sales of the book at time $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **number of sales** $S$ of a particular book **increases** with time. The **rate** of change of the number of sales is **proportional** to the **the square of the number of sales**.  *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *number of sales* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *number of sales squared.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}∝{S}^{2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the number of sales *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=k{S}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313737 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "344f1838-a027-5fd5-82da-17e9b4fcac2b" :knowledge-point/title "Further Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2684a100-c840-5ef6-935a-64b1ec4dfd43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-k\\sqrt{V},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313739} {:answer/id #uuid "1e7230c3-5b17-5ad4-964f-a41bc3794ac0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k\\sqrt{V},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313740} {:answer/id #uuid "cf60eb93-3667-5bd5-8af4-3f31821f3038" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-k{V}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313741} {:answer/id #uuid "73970c12-cb49-5223-aecf-0749c9c672ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k{V}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313742} {:answer/id #uuid "23426d91-83f7-5fd3-9d59-5571f7871632" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{V},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73970c12-cb49-5223-aecf-0749c9c672ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k{V}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313742} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f253a80-dc2e-5c9b-a29b-27ebd873e264" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b30d46be-646e-5361-a604-5fb66c710b5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-143034" :question/problem "The value $V$ of a particular art object increases with time. The rate of change of the object's value is proportional to the square of the value. What differential equation describes the change of the value at time $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **value** $V$ of a particular art object **increases** with time. The **rate** of change of the object's value is **proportional** to the **square of the value**.  *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *value* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *value squared.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}∝{V}^{2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the value *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k{V}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295874 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "344f1838-a027-5fd5-82da-17e9b4fcac2b" :knowledge-point/title "Further Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a4c60a8-f101-5eef-9c93-587751322eb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=k\\sqrt{v},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295876} {:answer/id #uuid "63a1febe-6a7a-5422-8c17-c5540c2e7e97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-k{v}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295877} {:answer/id #uuid "9188262e-9673-5eed-83b2-a5fd5cd3b6d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=k{v}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295878} {:answer/id #uuid "7f71007e-d2c5-54ea-bfc6-348221a38aa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{v}^{2}},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295879} {:answer/id #uuid "114d7be1-1926-558d-8724-31e887cac167" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{v}^{2}},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63a1febe-6a7a-5422-8c17-c5540c2e7e97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-k{v}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295877} :answer-field/id #uuid "27ffd44f-d95c-5b66-9de7-8b47da4773de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "000bf12e-faf9-50f0-869d-67748f8e8824" :question/math-academy-id "q-143036" :question/problem "The velocity $v$ of a stone falling through some water to the bottom of a lake decreases with time. The rate of change of the stone's velocity is proportional to the square of the velocity. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the velocity at time $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **velocity** $v$ of a stone falling through some water to the bottom of a lake **decreases** with time. The **rate** of change of the stone's velocity is **proportional** to the **square of the velocity**.  *\n\nWe note the following: \n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *velocity* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *velocity squared.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}∝{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the velocity *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-k{v}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281731 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "344f1838-a027-5fd5-82da-17e9b4fcac2b" :knowledge-point/title "Further Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "194579ae-4656-525a-a8b4-ac27ff23406c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=k{t}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281733} {:answer/id #uuid "93ec1bd4-735d-5a6a-9eca-90eeb6de59c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{t}^{2}},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281734} {:answer/id #uuid "4dd0b829-8111-5c4b-ba12-041c570a3ee1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=kt,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281735} {:answer/id #uuid "44752fbb-e7dd-5b05-a2af-d0b0d2fc0056" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=-k{t}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281736} {:answer/id #uuid "3a826549-7c98-5909-a33b-5ca56a8a9db3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=kM,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281737}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44752fbb-e7dd-5b05-a2af-d0b0d2fc0056" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=-k{t}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281736} :answer-field/id #uuid "19d5c9f7-1f25-5e29-95c0-c5ebf4acd9f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281732}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e05f1f82-64f3-5699-bd01-55ade5a30c00" :question/math-academy-id "q-143039" :question/problem "Mia won some money in the lottery and is now spending it. The amount of money $M$ in her bank account decreases at a rate that's proportional to the square of the time $t,$ in days, since she won.\nWhat differential equation describes the rate of change of the amount of money $M$ at time $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **amount** of the money $M$ **decreases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to the **square of the time** $t,$ in days.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *amount* of money varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *square of the time.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}∝{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the amount *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=-k{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295881 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d6e417b-e2a7-57f1-8dcd-4de5ff5d7e6b" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Inversely Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aefdcf67-bcdc-5ed9-b0c0-b392a37658bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{H},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295883} {:answer/id #uuid "08fff3d5-f006-5e51-b487-252567288d43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-kH,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295884} {:answer/id #uuid "0cb91c8b-41a8-5201-a728-dda49efb372f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=kH,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295885} {:answer/id #uuid "d0e1903b-8dd1-5d3e-990b-7e0192bf2bf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{t},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295886} {:answer/id #uuid "ce285e3c-2f8f-5986-9c6a-2c74be616204" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{H},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aefdcf67-bcdc-5ed9-b0c0-b392a37658bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{H},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295883} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9392e9a-f656-5daf-b678-018e74b9f8bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295882}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "91ea1276-29dd-5d34-8bf4-312e105d7100" :question/math-academy-id "q-143058" :question/problem "The height $H$ of a tree increases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the tree's height at that moment.\nWhat differential equation describes the rate of change of the height $H?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **height** $H$ of a tree **increases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the **tree's height** $H$ at that moment. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *height* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *tree's height.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}∝\\frac{1}{H}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the height *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{H}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281745 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d6e417b-e2a7-57f1-8dcd-4de5ff5d7e6b" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Inversely Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "346ee82a-dcbd-51a0-8153-38d4acb62d22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{V}^{2}},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281747} {:answer/id #uuid "bb438e04-a5a2-5861-a876-9c032f33c0a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{t}^{2}},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281748} {:answer/id #uuid "482444ac-cee1-5bf4-834f-d561d42178eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k{V}^{2},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281749} {:answer/id #uuid "d965a06d-d221-5a6d-9d38-9e71c5c4b693" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-k{V}^{2},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281750} {:answer/id #uuid "9690b205-4f85-5ab2-a542-6fadb7cff0ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{V}^{2}},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281751}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "346ee82a-dcbd-51a0-8153-38d4acb62d22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{V}^{2}},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281747} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe0841ff-f957-5d52-9440-28bb874017c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281746}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d16fe384-c4a2-5740-a6e6-81b73ba1228b" :question/math-academy-id "q-143061" :question/problem "The volume of water $V$ in a pool being filled increases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the square of the volume of water in the pool at that moment. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the volume of water $V$ in the pool?" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The  **volume of water** $V$  in a pool being filled **increases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the ** square of the volume of water** $V$ in the pool at that moment. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume of water* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *square of the volume.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}∝\\frac{1}{{V}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the volume of water *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{V}^{2}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281759 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d6e417b-e2a7-57f1-8dcd-4de5ff5d7e6b" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Inversely Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4cf587d-db87-57e5-9a06-a4b3afa3e174" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{t},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281761} {:answer/id #uuid "b5d87430-9de6-50b0-a49f-c9df03b20297" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=kt,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281762} {:answer/id #uuid "6574d030-f380-59b8-aa8b-254c8f43b957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{C},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281763} {:answer/id #uuid "042ca691-6da5-522d-9381-8c694df7c921" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=-kC,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281764} {:answer/id #uuid "c1bc5056-30f6-5a25-a4fb-b5a739b891e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{t},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1bc5056-30f6-5a25-a4fb-b5a739b891e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{t},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281765} :answer-field/id #uuid "35de313a-7ad5-57b8-9455-ebbcd948f70c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f6d27ee8-7a9e-59cc-aba5-99cecf01eb5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-143081" :question/problem "The charge $C$ of a phone battery decreases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the time $t$ spent charging the smartphone. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the charge $C$ of the phone battery?" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **charge** $C$ of a phone battery **decreases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the **time** $t$ spent charging the smartphone.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *charge* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *time.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}∝\\frac{1}{t}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the number *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{t}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281766 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d6e417b-e2a7-57f1-8dcd-4de5ff5d7e6b" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Inversely Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab0a2484-9701-5ebf-9683-ac60974c8b6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-k{t}^{2},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281768} {:answer/id #uuid "9160920f-9320-5c23-a35b-5ff41cbc2b71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{S}^{2}},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281769} {:answer/id #uuid "9dcd7858-cbe0-5076-8b17-8092abaf53a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{t},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281770} {:answer/id #uuid "8fbac362-195b-5db7-b1fb-57506ad7b25e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{S}^{2}},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281771} {:answer/id #uuid "3815e13d-52cc-566d-8290-274d1e7a3f3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{t}^{2}},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3815e13d-52cc-566d-8290-274d1e7a3f3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{t}^{2}},\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281772} :answer-field/id #uuid "088bf1a8-a840-5d18-8346-7195ea4d0130" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d4f83b6d-0b59-5cc8-9341-b97fb9c62549" :question/math-academy-id "q-143094" :question/problem "The speed $S$ of an object decreases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the square of the travel time $t.$ What differential equation describes the rate of change of the speed $S?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **speed** $S$ of an object **decreases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the **square of the travel time** $t.$*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *speed* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *square of the travel time.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}∝\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the speed *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{t}^{2}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281777 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a247a205-ca5c-5079-820c-cfa34b6f53db" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Constant Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa989acd-9827-50f5-abe0-a1c87b056cb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa989acd-9827-50f5-abe0-a1c87b056cb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281779} :answer-field/id #uuid "67e81cb6-0174-51cd-af4e-179781d14adb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "01d0d014-2d16-55f2-87e9-0d2babf92850" :question/math-academy-id "q-353449" :question/problem "The number of completed online course enrollments increases by $18$ enrollments every hour during a promotional event. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the number of enrollments $E(t)?$ Assume that the time $t$ is measured in hours.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}E}{\\text{d}t}=$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If the number of enrollments is $E,$ then the rate of change of the number of enrollments is $\\frac{\\text{d}E}{\\text{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in hours.\n\nSince $18$ new enrollments are added every hour, the rate of change of the number of enrollments is given by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}E}{\\text{d}t}=18.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313744 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a247a205-ca5c-5079-820c-cfa34b6f53db" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Constant Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3631dab-9cc0-588c-b563-72fe8cee6bab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313746}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3631dab-9cc0-588c-b563-72fe8cee6bab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313746} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa6a76ed-6418-5cda-8e27-b1d632041b70" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "952f2d49-202a-52d4-9191-44f2632fec2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-353452" :question/problem "A reservoir releases $14$ cubic meters of water every hour. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the volume of water $V(t)$ remaining in the reservoir? Assume that the time $t$ is measured in hours.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If the volume of water remaining is $V,$ then the rate of change of the volume is $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in hours.\n\nSince $14$ cubic meters of water are released each hour, the rate of change of the volume is given by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-14.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is negative because the volume of water is *decreasing*."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313747 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a247a205-ca5c-5079-820c-cfa34b6f53db" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Constant Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "692c0428-f099-54bd-9fb1-f2212760fd68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}E}{\\text{d}t}=k,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313749} {:answer/id #uuid "ab933d8b-61e5-5b1e-94ab-c3bee5382d22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}E}{\\text{d}t}=-k,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313750} {:answer/id #uuid "601964eb-2d0f-57a4-8aa5-bf28e86a3e72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}E}{\\text{d}t}=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313751} {:answer/id #uuid "525a94c7-b1bf-5165-8f37-6ebb93344c1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}E}{\\text{d}t}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313752} {:answer/id #uuid "2bc30fe2-1363-5a61-bbbe-1e5858508b3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}E}{\\text{d}t}=e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "525a94c7-b1bf-5165-8f37-6ebb93344c1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}E}{\\text{d}t}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313752} :answer-field/id #uuid "87b0ec2c-eb5d-57fd-b5c4-3aebc8c485d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313748}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ee85b646-b59f-5b4a-a6c1-462d95d72046" :question/math-academy-id "q-38308" :question/problem "During the winter months, the energy consumption of an average household increases by $1\\,\\text{kWh}$ every week. What differential equation describes the rate of change of energy consumption $E(t)$ (measured in $\\text{kWh}$) of an average household during these months? Assume that the time $t$ is measured in weeks." :question/worked-solution "If the energy consumption of an average household is $E\\,\\text{kWh},$ then the rate of change of the energy consumption is $\\frac{\\text{d}E}{\\text{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in weeks.\n\nSince an additional $1\\,\\text{kWh}$ is consumed every week, the rate of change of the energy consumption is given by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}E}{\\text{d}t}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295894 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "255dee12-5f44-536b-b679-5a894843395d" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3fc1e2a-d7b9-55f3-a8db-4948caf4a01d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=tA" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295896} {:answer/id #uuid "ee7dacc6-1e01-5aed-9065-745a0c1b937c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295897} {:answer/id #uuid "67be357a-be3d-5b48-8131-6c31c67bb821" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=k+A,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295898} {:answer/id #uuid "447c66c6-c98e-5588-85c8-7e7329e54077" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=k-A,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295899} {:answer/id #uuid "26ae4d63-2052-5085-b656-7698d7989d9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=kA,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295900}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26ae4d63-2052-5085-b656-7698d7989d9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=kA,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295900} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ebaaa1d-ab07-54c5-a725-cf8b3eccfce2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e3c5c93-86d8-5789-925d-7bd84acb1b9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-38588" :question/problem "The number of ants in an anthill increases at a rate that is proportional to the number of ants at the moment. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the number of ants, $A,$ at time $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **number of ants** in an anthill **increases** at a **rate** that is **proportional** to the **number of ants** at the moment.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *number of ants* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *number of ants.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}∝A.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the number of ants *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=kA.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281787 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "255dee12-5f44-536b-b679-5a894843395d" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00d87778-9f6d-572f-ba24-efb2b01987c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k+V,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281789} {:answer/id #uuid "12cc5a2f-4920-5e47-b94d-215b9f53fb7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=kV,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281790} {:answer/id #uuid "7879ec9f-de30-5298-b5ad-eb700ebabf2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=tV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281791} {:answer/id #uuid "32a89d93-5177-5162-bd49-ea2e6085aa05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k-V,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281792} {:answer/id #uuid "999d8f50-9a49-5221-87cf-61d7ddd7de1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=V" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281793}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12cc5a2f-4920-5e47-b94d-215b9f53fb7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=kV,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281790} :answer-field/id #uuid "d75ca6da-7fe4-5e39-8c9d-57a413cefe1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1d093bd1-c5c7-53da-bfe8-604b0e2c79b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-38589" :question/problem "Some people in a community contracted an unknown virus. The number of people infected by the virus increases at a rate that is proportional to the number of people infected by the virus. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the number of people who contracted the virus, $V,$ at time $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **number of people** infected by the virus **increases** at a **rate** that is **proportional** to the **number of people** infected by the virus.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *number of people* infected by the virus varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *number of people* infected. In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}∝V.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the number of people infected by the virus  *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=kV.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281794 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "255dee12-5f44-536b-b679-5a894843395d" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25f500c2-13a0-5c3f-87db-288c7155abea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=kC,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281796} {:answer/id #uuid "e2fc3eb2-1990-5713-a4cf-b8c49538ffd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=k-C,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281797} {:answer/id #uuid "bc3878d5-5ebd-5d99-bdf6-d73532122c80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=-C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281798} {:answer/id #uuid "01962f7b-8aeb-530c-8eec-a68c98758afd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=-kC,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281799} {:answer/id #uuid "8159d214-91a2-5e15-8edc-c3af37bb63d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=-tC" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01962f7b-8aeb-530c-8eec-a68c98758afd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=-kC,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281799} :answer-field/id #uuid "543853c3-44cd-5704-be17-3bb064599976" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "69c85e0b-7e0b-5837-bb9c-2bbb4965d5f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-38591" :question/problem "When heated, the concentration $C(t)$ of oxygen in water decreases at a rate that's proportional to the concentration of oxygen in the water at that moment. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the oxygen concentration at time $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*When heated, the **concentration** $C(t)$ of oxygen in water **decreases** at a **rate** that is **proportional** to the **concentration** of oxygen in the water at that moment.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *concentration* of oxygen in water varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *concentration* of oxygen in the water. In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}∝C.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the concentration of oxygen *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=-kC.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129612 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "255dee12-5f44-536b-b679-5a894843395d" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "800bd6db-d401-59b0-ad53-9c73e0feac24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\frac{dh}{dt} = h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127467} {:answer/id #uuid "ac858183-00ae-547d-bb2b-84f7bb96862f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dh}{dt} = kh, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127468} {:answer/id #uuid "0dd5b544-812c-5607-81bc-f6cfa123f680" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dh}{dt} =-kh, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127469} {:answer/id #uuid "409fc9a1-aafd-5a98-8adf-3eed59fdd145" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dh}{dt} = th" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127470} {:answer/id #uuid "432edeec-de08-5435-8494-c17a7330128f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dh}{dt} = k + h, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac858183-00ae-547d-bb2b-84f7bb96862f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dh}{dt} = kh, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127468} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dabb5f3-c693-5c5f-9113-15ad835af72f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127466}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "627801d7-ccc0-5da8-aa59-a0766c9e2ba0" :question/math-academy-id "q-38595" :question/problem "Peter has planted a tree. The height $h$ of the tree increases at a rate that is proportional to the height of the tree. What differential equation describes the rate of growth of the height of the tree at time $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **height** of the tree **increases** at a **rate** that is **proportional** to the **height** of the tree.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *height* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *height.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}∝h.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the height *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=kh.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313754 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d6e417b-e2a7-57f1-8dcd-4de5ff5d7e6b" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Inversely Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25897e8d-b758-5a76-8f52-e2842d06d3a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{t}{B}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313756} {:answer/id #uuid "d23ea2a3-f89e-574d-bfb0-56e39f72adba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=k+\\frac{1}{B},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313757} {:answer/id #uuid "3c3be60f-7d3d-5c25-8e1a-2e04879ed5b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{B},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313758} {:answer/id #uuid "b50f87fe-bd0e-5f67-a879-df8f18b948ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{B}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313759} {:answer/id #uuid "ae957a24-975a-515d-a05e-0bf046ea696c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=k-\\frac{1}{B},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c3be60f-7d3d-5c25-8e1a-2e04879ed5b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{B},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313758} :answer-field/id #uuid "aef1a381-71aa-5859-8fa4-51baa76310af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3b568e1f-fb45-5c85-9ad6-367daab9d1a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-38596" :question/problem "A bug population  $B$ increases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the bug population at a given time. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the bug population $B?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*A **bug population** $B$ **increases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the **bug population** $B$  at a given time.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *bug population* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *bug population.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}∝\\frac{1}{B}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the bug population *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{B}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129749 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "344f1838-a027-5fd5-82da-17e9b4fcac2b" :knowledge-point/title "Further Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2833a79-c64c-58e2-841e-294e5d5933dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dV}{dt} =-k\\sqrt{V}, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128289} {:answer/id #uuid "ee45cbbe-91ad-5e3a-b2b0-082969f6a84e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dV}{dt} = \\frac{k}{V^{2}}, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128290} {:answer/id #uuid "416f07cf-1706-50fa-8b17-6daf757e4753" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dV}{dt} =-kV^{2}, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128291} {:answer/id #uuid "545a0df9-1298-5759-a415-bd983883c823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dV}{dt} =-kV, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128292} {:answer/id #uuid "70350c1e-f017-56cc-afb3-45ef43d59f4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dV}{dt} = kV^{2}, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128293}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "416f07cf-1706-50fa-8b17-6daf757e4753" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dV}{dt} =-kV^{2}, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128291} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1d3821f-04cc-5ace-979c-d8f8b97466ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d2a8b5ef-a26c-5387-8984-3108c19f3b8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-51247" :question/problem "The value $V$ of a particular car decreases with time. The rate of change of the car's value is proportional to the square of the value. What differential equation describes the change of the value at time $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **value** $V$ of a particular car **decreases** with time. The **rate** of change of the car's value is **proportional** to the **value squared**.  *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *value* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *value squared.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}∝{V}^{2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the value *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-k{V}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129750 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a247a205-ca5c-5079-820c-cfa34b6f53db" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Constant Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1e6375c-6122-5dfb-a802-922e2f433f89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt} = 100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128295} {:answer/id #uuid "b351ac2e-260a-5509-944c-936c541c5041" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt} = 100k, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128296} {:answer/id #uuid "ba6d0fcd-3941-5f82-8c30-398358e59116" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt} = e^{100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128297} {:answer/id #uuid "ff89cf08-e9b9-5480-9c81-69965f9889eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt} = 100 + P" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128298} {:answer/id #uuid "4fed2ed0-c125-5966-a31b-c6784921b876" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt} = 100P" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1e6375c-6122-5dfb-a802-922e2f433f89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt} = 100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128295} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed35efd3-d2cc-5835-a2a3-c99425f3caf4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1cbb7150-61e1-50bf-8ac9-84dfe58c7fcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-51251" :question/problem "The population of pines in a forest increases by $100$ trees every year. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the population $P(t)$ of the pines? Assume that the time $t$ is measured in years." :question/worked-solution "If the pines population is $P,$ then the rate of change of the population is $\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in years.\n\nSince $100$ new pines are added every year, the rate of change of the population of the pines is given by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=100.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281808 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "255dee12-5f44-536b-b679-5a894843395d" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51f8e7f5-b543-5151-b6a5-e1edb10f17f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{B},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281810} {:answer/id #uuid "a7f47d35-0b83-5c74-b3b4-3ba60752e64f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=-kB,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281811} {:answer/id #uuid "42fe3c71-23a7-53ff-bd6e-622ba986ab34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{B},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281812} {:answer/id #uuid "b75c7173-c53f-5ebb-973b-4050d1f0febb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281813} {:answer/id #uuid "46aadb23-32a7-52ca-8802-5e4bac17e3db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=k-\\frac{1}{B},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7f47d35-0b83-5c74-b3b4-3ba60752e64f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=-kB,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281811} :answer-field/id #uuid "99d16e8d-cf11-5676-87a1-e8b7d6cc7fb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2cfb46d3-f6f4-5c4b-810e-a81336544d7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-51254" :question/problem "The number of bacteria in a particular crop at any given moment decreases at a rate that is proportional to the number of bacteria at that moment.\nWhat differential equation describes the rate of change of the number of bacteria $B$ at time $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **number of bacteria** in a particular crop at any given moment **decreases** at a **rate** that is **proportional** to **the number of bacteria** at that moment..* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *number of bacteria* in a particular crop varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *number of bacteria* at the moment. In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}∝B.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the number of bacteria *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=-kB.\n$$"}]}
